<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Bing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Bing was a governor of Shu under the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in the 3rd century BCE, traditionally credited with building the Dujiangyan irrigation system on the Min river near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. The system divided and channelled the river without a dam, controlling floods and irrigating the Chengdu plain, and it is still in use. He is revered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, together with his son Erlang, at the Two Kings Temple at Dujiangyan.</p></div>
